{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792435332,"nonce":"/xDsHj6sJ1+PnRQUnWsth8SyZXA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABYW0ClQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/169","origin":"github"},"title":"Proposal: reuse=\"auto\" -- rebuild a kept preconditioner only when the iterations say it pays","message":"## Proposal\n\nAdd one more value to the preconditioner keyword `reuse=`: **`reuse=\"auto\"`** -- keep the setup across solves and rebuild it only when it has gone stale enough that a rebuild pays for itself.\n\n```python\nM = jno.precond.amg(nullspace=\"rigid\", reuse=\"auto\")\nfor p in design_iterations:\n    u = fem.solve(p=p, x0=u, linear=jno.solve.cg(), precond=M)\n```\n\n`reuse=` (replacing `.cached()`) and `amg(nullspace=...)` arrive with an upcoming change; `reuse=` takes `False` (rebuild every solve), `True` (build once, keep) and `k` (rebuild every k-th solve). None of them follows how fast the operator actually drifts: `True` slowly loses iterations, `k` rebuilds on a fixed clock whether or not it is needed, `False` pays the setup on every solve.\n\n## Evidence\n\n3-D St.Venant-Kirchhoff elasticity, P1, ~100k DOFs, CG + `amg(nullspace=\"rigid\")`, a load parameter swept over 10 parametric solves (warm-started from the previous solution, as in an optimisation loop). CG iterations per solve, all Newton steps together:\n\n| step | 0 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 7 | 9 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| kept from the first solve (`reuse=True`) | 42 | 46 | 48 | 73 | 79 | 84 |\n| rebuilt before every solve | 42 | 42 | 43 | 63 | 62 | 62 |\n\n- The kept hierarchy degrades slowly: +35 % iterations after 10 steps.\n- One rebuild cost about 3x a solve on the test GPU, and saves ~10-25 % of a solve per step at this drift -- so rebuilding every step loses, keeping forever loses slowly, and the break-even depends on the problem.\n- A rebuild no longer costs a recompile: a rebuilt spec of the same shapes reuses the compiled solve with its new arrays.\n\n## Suggested rule\n\nThe signal is already recorded: the Krylov iteration count of each solve.\n\n- After a (re)build, remember the iteration count `k0` of the first solve that used it.\n- Track the excess `sum(k_i - k0)` of later solves, converted to time with the measured cost per iteration.\n- Rebuild once that accumulated excess exceeds the measured setup time of the last build (both known after the first build).\n\nThis needs no new concept from the user and no traced code: the decision is made between solves, on concrete numbers. Open questions:\n\n1. Newton-step count changes with the problem, not with the preconditioner (step 5 above jumps in both rows). Normalise by Newton steps, or compare against the iteration count a fresh build *would* have needed (unknown without building)?\n2. A fixed ratio threshold (rebuild when `k_i \u003e 1.5 k0`) is simpler but would have rebuilt on the step-5 jump, which was the problem getting harder, not the hierarchy going stale.\n3. Inside a march (one scan per run), the decision can only be made between chunks -- the same constraint `reuse=k` already has there.","files":null}]}